  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday Night through Monday)\nIssued at 1147 PM CDT Mon Sep 7 2026\n\nThe ridge will tend to flatten through the day Wednesday as a \nstrong longwave trough develops across the Upper Midwest/Great \nLakes region. The associated cold front will move through the \ncentral plains early in the day and approach our CWA sometime late\nWednesday afternoon or evening. Ahead of the front, another hot \nday is likely with temperatures between 100 and 105 for most of \nthe area. Additional heat headlines will be needed. Diurnal \nconvection is possible again, mainly over the higher terrain of NW\nAR. However, higher precipitation chances will occur with the \narrival of the cold front Wednesday evening and overnight... \nparticularly across NE OK and NW AR.\n\nShower/thunderstorm chances will persist along the frontal \nboundary as it progresses south Wednesday night into Thursday. The\nfront is expected to stall during the day Thursday, potentially \nin the vicinity of I-40 or further into SE OK/W AR. This will \nprovide at least low precipitation chances until the front lifts \nback north of the area on Friday. In addition to increasing rain \npotential, the front will also help cool the area off. High \ntemperatures in the upper 80s and lower 90s are currently forecast\nnorth of I-40 on Thursday. Any areas that remain ahead of the \nfront will likely continue to experience well above average \ntemperatures, but still slightly cooler due to increased cloud \ncover (mid-upper 90s). A shortwave moving through the region on \nFriday may promote additional shower/storm development, especially\nacross NE OK and W AR.\n\nModel variability increases going into the weekend, but there is \nsome agreement that another cold front will at least approach the \narea on Saturday. Precipitation potential will likely hinge on \njust how far south the front makes it, but the NBM currently \nprovides fairly widespread 20-40 percent PoPs during this time \nframe. Southerly flow and drier conditions could return early next\nweek before another, potentially stronger, cold front impacts the\nregion. More details will become available over the coming days, \nbut the main takeaway is that a more active weather pattern seems \nto be shaping up for the area beginning mid to late this week and \npersisting into the next. Increasing rain and front activity will\nalso tend to keep temperatures in the 90s or lower Friday and \nbeyond.",
